ia `npm config set prefix ~/.npm-global`. |\n19: | **npm Global Binaries** | `~/.npm-global/bin` | `/home` | 220 GB NVMe | Symlinks/wrappers in user home PATH. |\n20: | **npm Project Packages\n<truncated 1982 bytes>\n databases and Telethon auth sessions. |\n37: | **CockroachDB Store** | `<project>/tools/cockroach-data/` | `/home` | 220 GB NVMe | Dedicated store path under `/home`. |\n38: | **PostgreSQL (default)** | `/var/lib/postgresql/` | `/` | 30 GB Root | System service default (can redirect to `/home/postgres_data`). |\n39: | **System Logs** | `/var/log/journal/` | `/` | 30 GB Root | Systemd journal (rotated and clamped). |\n40: \n41: ---\n42: \n43: ## B. ROOT-DISK LEAK REPORT (Every vector that can write to the 30 GB root disk)\n44: \n45: 1. **Unset TMPDIR (PROVEN & MITIGATED):**\n46:    - *Risk:* Without `TMPDIR`, Go, Node, Python, and AGY language servers write schemas and temp files to `/tmp` on root disk.\n47:    - *Evidence:* Live test revealed `/tmp/unleash-repo-schema-v1-...json` (463 KB) on root.\n48:    - *Mitigation:* Set `export TMPDIR=\"/home/azureuser/tmp\"` in `~/.bashrc` and `~/.profile`. Retest verified 0 bytes written to root `/tmp`.\n49: 2. **Global sudo pip installations:**\n50:    - *Risk:* Running `sudo pip install` places wheels in `/usr/local/lib/python3.x/dist-packages/` on root.\n51:    - *Mitigation:* Enforce `pip install --user` or virtual environments (`python3 -m venv`).\n52: 3. **Global npm installations without user prefix:**\n53:    - *Risk:* Running `sudo npm install -g` places packages in `/usr/local/lib/node_modules/` on root.\n54:    - *Mitigation:* Enforce `npm config set prefix '~/.npm-global'` on Azure.\n55: 4. **PostgreSQL Default Data Directory:**\n56:    - *Risk:* Standard PostgreSQL stores tables in `/var/lib/postgresql/17/main/` on root.\n57:    - *Mitigation:* Symlink or set `data_directory = '/home/postgres_data'` in `postgresql.conf`.\n58: 5. **APT Package Cache:**\n59:    - *Risk:* Large apt downloads remain in `/var/cache/apt/archives/`.\n60:    - *Mitigation:* Run `sudo apt-get clean` after system installations.\nThe above content does NOT show the entire file contents. If you need to view any lines of the file which were not shown to complete your task, call this tool again to view those lines.\n","truncated_fields":["content"]}
